# A Blow to Humaneness
Tamil title: மக்கட்பண்புக்கு மண்டையில் அடி

Not merely one or two, nor even hundreds or thousands, but tens of thousands—the very thought makes the heart shudder—of Jain elders were impaled upon stakes erected along the roads and killed. Sambandar, who thus demonstrated the close relationship between Saivism and love, set out from Madurai with his band of devotees and reached a town called Bodhimangai amid Saivite chants.

Bodhimangai had a large Buddhist population. Its people did not like the manner in which Sambandar's party entered their town with Saivite clamour. The Buddhists therefore went with their leader, Buddhanandi, to meet the party and said, “Why these emblems of victory? Have you defeated us in debate? They ought to be sounded only after victory in debate!”

Did Sambandar's party agree? Certainly not. After all, were they not the very measure of loving-kindness? Would they consent to debate? Was it not the proof of Saivite love to provoke a quarrel and force a fight? Anxious that Saivism's loving-kindness should not deviate by even an atom, Sambandar's followers reportedly shouted as soon as they heard Buddhanandi challenge them to debate: “This man must be destroyed; otherwise he will cause harm.”

Just look at Saivism's loving-kindness. Sambandar's party entered a town belonging to others, loudly proclaiming a religion alien to that town. The Buddhists addressed them according to ordinary civil convention: “This is not proper for you. We are Buddhists and you are Saivites. If you wish to sound your Saivite cries here, in our predominantly Buddhist town and before us, proper conduct requires you first to debate us and defeat us.” If a group grew enraged with them for saying this and declared, “They must be destroyed; otherwise they will cause harm,” could that group have any connection whatever with love? Could anyone regard it as knowing the rules of civil conduct? Could anyone say that it possessed even the smallest measure of humaneness? I ask readers to reflect upon these questions.

“Let us debate.”

“Impossible. We shall pick a quarrel.”

“Let us speak with human decency.”

“Impossible. We shall strike you on the head.”

“Let us speak rationally.”

“Oh, is that so? We shall attack arrogantly.”

“Let us converse with love.”

“How could we? We shall beat you until your bones break.”

It was in precisely this fashion that the exchange took place between the Buddhists of Bodhimangai and Sambandar's party. Anyone who calmly and rationally compares the Buddhists' honourable words with the Saivite party's abusive words will readily understand the nature of the love buried within Saivism.

Can any difference be found between a man who lies stupefied by excessive drink and forcibly drags those who approach him into a quarrel, and the treatment of Buddhists who invited them to debate—abusing them with every word that came to the mouth, striking them on the head and killing them? Consider it.

Some may wonder: did Sambandar's party truly strike the Buddhists who challenged them to debate on the head and kill them? That this is the truth emerges not from reasons offered by me, but from Sekkizhar's own words.

Sekkizhar says that Sambandar was informed of the Buddhists' challenge and graciously declared, “What is this! Very well! Let us test Buddhanandi's ability in debate.” Meanwhile, as the “crisis” intensified, the man who wrote down Sambandar's *Tevaram* hymns began with the words “the Buddhists and Jains, the impalers” and finished by singing, “the five letters themselves shall be the weapon.” At once, lightning allegedly struck Buddhanandi, split his skull and killed him. Upon hearing of the event, Sambandar reportedly said, “This occurred by fate; chant the name of Siva,” and ordered his followers to do so. This is the episode Sekkizhar relates.

“This fellow? He spoke like that?”

“Yes, master. He is the very man who stood at the street corner speaking that way!”

“Very well! We shall have to see just how clever he is.”

“Until now, no one has ever dared speak so boldly about the master.”

“Indeed! What can be done? Would all this happen if these fellows had been kept in their proper place? Hey! Who is there? Do you know this ‘gentleman’ was standing at the street corner saying something?”

“I know, sir. With the anger I felt, I would have dealt with him then and there—”

“Nothing has been lost even now. Show the ‘gentleman’ a little ‘respect’ and send him away.”

We have heard that conversations of this sort, followed by corresponding deeds, occur on some estates and zamindari domains in our country.

There consequently seems no difference between this manner of conversation and Sambandar's response upon hearing of the Buddhist challenge:

“What is this!”

“Very well!”

“Let us test Buddhanandi's ability in debate.”

Nor is there any difference between the deeds following such an exchange and the reported episode in which Sambandar's scribe sang some verse and caused lightning to strike Buddhanandi.

Had Sambandar believed it proper to debate the Buddhists who challenged him, he would never have mocked the news with expressions such as “What is this?” and “Very well!” No one can deny that these expressions are used when one intends ridicule. They arise from an anger-tinged laugh at seeing or hearing something one dislikes; no other meaning can be assigned to them. Any attempt to do so would produce an incongruous interpretation.

It is therefore quite clear that Sambandar did not wish to debate the Buddhists. It is equally plain that these very words of his served as the incitement to kill Buddhanandi.

The further story that the Buddhists created some other emergency after challenging Sambandar, prompting his scribe to sing and make lightning fall upon Buddhanandi, cannot possibly be true. Leave aside the unfiltered falsehood that, upon hearing a song, lightning hidden among the clouds rushed down and killed Buddhanandi. It is evident that Buddhists who called Sambandar to debate would not immediately have provoked a brawl and created an emergency.

Had the Buddhists intended such a thing, they could easily have done it and would have done so. Sekkizhar himself says that Bodhimangai was a predominantly Buddhist place; it would not have been difficult for them to provoke Sambandar's party after it entered their town. But they did not wish to do so. Although the visitors were their religious opponents, the Buddhists understood that treating them peacefully was the conduct proper to humaneness, and so invited them to debate.

It was Sambandar's party, unable to accept that invitation, which became enraged upon hearing “Come to debate” and cried, “He must be destroyed; otherwise he will cause harm.” None but a group incapable of debating its challengers and winning victory for its position would turn a challenge to debate into a quarrel.

Sekkizhar himself also provides the evidence that Sambandar's scribe struck Buddhanandi on the head and killed him. He describes the devotee who split Buddhanandi in two:

“Before them, the peerless devotee who reduced to powder
the dark head of the Buddhist who obstructed the sacred emblems...”

This is Sekkizhar's praise of the “devotee” who killed Buddhanandi. In this verse, *ethiril anbar* means “a devotee without an opponent”—that is, a man of such physical strength that no one could stand against him. Otherwise, there would be no reason for those words to appear there.

If Sambandar's scribe truly possessed the spiritual power to make lightning strike Buddhanandi, the verse might have called him *arul-nirai anbar*, “a devotee filled with grace,” instead of *ethiril anbar*. If some versifiers imagined that the two words *arul nirai* would spoil the metre, they could have combined them as *arunirai*, just as *ethir illatha*, “without an opponent,” was compressed into the single word *ethiril*. The structure of the verse would not thereby have been damaged in the least.

We must therefore examine such puranas in order to ask whether intelligent people can follow a religion that permits cruelties so far beyond humaneness that they cannot be conceived even in dreams.

9 February 1947
